In this lesson, we'll look at how the two parameters in the line equation y equals m x plus c control the shape and position of the line. First, let's talk about m, the gradient. The bigger the absolute value of m, the steeper the line gets, pushing it closer to vertical. The sign tells you the direction. A positive m rises as you read left to right, a negative m falls, and a zero gives you a perfectly flat horizontal line. If you keep c fixed and slide m from negative three up to positive three, the line simply rotates around the fixed point on the y-axis, like a hinge. Next, let's look at c, the y-intercept. Changing c slides the whole line straight up or straight down without altering its steepness at all. If you keep m fixed and vary c, you get a whole family of perfectly parallel lines. A couple of nice connections to remember. The line always passes through the point on the y-axis where y equals c, no matter what m is. And two lines are parallel only if their m values match, and perpendicular only if their m values multiply to negative one. Now let's see it in action.